I worked on the very first Situated Activities, helped map initial stakeholders, and contributed to the rest of the Foundation part as well as the early Specification. The Situated Activity of brushing your teeth started as a stand-in or a literary device when discussing our project, and was filled out as one of the first drafts. Due to an absence of better ideas, we went along with it. The midterm presentation became a wake up call, after which it became even more clear that the problem to be solved, that is, losing track while brushing your teeth, is for all intents and purposes not a problem that lends itself to a technological solution. It is a very repetitive workflow that you have been doing twice every single day of your life starting at the age of 0, when your parents did it for you. When your motor skills become unreliable, you need someone to help you again. However, when the basic sense of where you are and what you are doing - leaves while you are brushing your teeth, you are likely to find it back after just a quick glance in the mirror or through feeling the toothbrush in your mouth. We therefore consider this problem not a great candidate for Socio-Cognitive Engineering.

Earlier this year I designed an operating system for the first smartphone that my grandmother ever used. A modern OS contains countless obstacles to a person with regressive cognitive decline. These are obstacles that prevent you from staying in contact with your loved ones. Humans are social creatures, and if we are lonely we suffer. Yet even when you have people all around you, you can still be lonely. I started developing a parallel version of our xWiki around introducing residents of a care home to one another. I worked on it alone until a point where it could be pitched to my teammates as a new, complete concept which would allow us to tackle more diverse problems involving human factors and needs. This involved establishing a skeleton of the full SCE cycle. Since many of my teammates were working on the project remotely, I created a video to help sell the new idea to my teammates. After an initial green light from the team and from the professors, we switched gears and worked on polishing the new concept. The originally written xWiki articles were kept as well.

//Note to reader: this paragraph is an unformatted list of contributions. //Some other parts I was also involved in include xWiki-preview, a VSCodium plugin that I put together for rendering xWiki articles locally. I assisted Roham with the creation of xwiki-sync, our script that enables us to collaborate on the xWiki contents using git. I cleaned up the paths of our wiki ("sdf" becomes "1 Foundations"), and collaborated with my teammates on the design of the summative evaluation, the experiment we would carry out in the ideal world. I designed both the old and the new prototypes for the cognitive evaluation. I represented my group in the remote feedback session on 13 October. I created and presented a part of the final presentation. I created the cognitive walkthrough video for the presentation and the Brightspace submission.

Collaboration with the team has been overall positive. However, our work would have been more efficient if we had met more in person (instead of part-online).

Most of all, I learned from and enjoyed the lecture on cognition. In general, the concepts covered in this course are extremely relevant if you want to create a good user experience for everyone (accessibility). It has been helpful to think about why we make certain decisions, and to read about the research that has been carried out in the fields related to SCE. I have to admit that the countless tables that we have had to fill out still seem like an obstacle that still needs to be smoothed out. //Imagine: what if we simply had a Postgres schema?// Then instead of endless copy-pasting you could just insert a new row, in other words, a new entity into the appropriate table, and voila, a new situated activity has been created. You would not have to worry about IDs getting mismatched either (foreign keys take care of this). It is not difficult to flatten such a database into a linear report either. I hope this idea at least gets considered for a future iteration of this course.